WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:02.730
All right, in this next section,

2
00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.809
we're gonna be talking about ICV effects cinematography

3
00:00:05.809 --> 00:00:08.330
and its considerations and techniques.

4
00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:10.369
All right, here's some of the guidelines

5
00:00:10.369 --> 00:00:13.050
and a quick overview of what we'll be talking about next.

6
00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:14.570
So we're gonna be taking a look

7
00:00:14.570 --> 00:00:17.809
at understanding pixel pitch and pixel density,

8
00:00:17.809 --> 00:00:22.899
visual acuity distances, power considerations and flicker,

9
00:00:22.899 --> 00:00:25.219
frame rates and refresh rates,

10
00:00:25.219 --> 00:00:28.019
rolling shutters versus global shutters,

11
00:00:28.019 --> 00:00:30.219
shutter angle and shutter speed,

12
00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:34.299
wire mitigation, aperture and depth of field,

13
00:00:34.299 --> 00:00:39.020
optical low pass filters and neutral density filters.

14
00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:42.859
So what is pixel pitch and what is pixel density?

15
00:00:42.859 --> 00:00:45.359
Well, pixel pitch is essentially,

16
00:00:45.359 --> 00:00:47.780
it is a millimeter measurement of the space

17
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.460
between the centers of each individual LED pixel

18
00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:53.500
within a video wall.

19
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:55.100
The pixel density on the other hand

20
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.820
is the number of pixels in a given specific screen area.

21
00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:00.619
Pixel density helps to define

22
00:01:00.619 --> 00:01:02.619
the recommended viewing distance

23
00:01:02.619 --> 00:01:06.739
in order to have a satisfactory viewing experience.

24
00:01:06.739 --> 00:01:10.819
In the Nant and LA R&D stages

25
00:01:10.819 --> 00:01:14.359
equipped with the Rho Black Pearl II panels,

26
00:01:14.359 --> 00:01:16.659
along here with the Latte stage,

27
00:01:16.659 --> 00:01:19.799
which is also running the Black Pearl II panels,

28
00:01:19.799 --> 00:01:23.599
it has a pixel pitch of 2.8 millimeters,

29
00:01:23.599 --> 00:01:25.879
which is actually pretty decent.

30
00:01:25.879 --> 00:01:29.920
There are new panels that are getting smaller pixel pitch,

31
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.359
and you can have panels that have larger pixel pitch.

32
00:01:32.359 --> 00:01:35.280
It all just depends upon your stage and studio size

33
00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.400
as well as to where you'd be needing to place the camera

34
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.579
in order to properly film this.

35
00:01:40.579 --> 00:01:42.319
So if we talk about that,

36
00:01:42.319 --> 00:01:47.180
we're actually describing visual acuity distances.

37
00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.260
So when we factor the panel size

38
00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.620
and the pixel pitch together,

39
00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:53.799
this determines the number of pixels per panel.

40
00:01:53.799 --> 00:01:57.840
For example, a pixel pitch of 2.6 millimeters

41
00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:00.400
on a 500 by 500 millimeter panel

42
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.379
will hold 192 by 192 pixels,

43
00:02:04.379 --> 00:02:08.199
or 36,834 pixels total.

44
00:02:08.199 --> 00:02:11.599
Now, if you're dealing with the 2.8 millimeter panels

45
00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:13.960
like we have at our facilities,

46
00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:19.250
I believe ours are 172 by 172 pixels each.

47
00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:22.370
Now, the size of the panel and its pixel pitch

48
00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:26.949
determines the level of visual acuity at various distances.

49
00:02:26.949 --> 00:02:31.090
So wherever the camera or the viewer will be placed.

50
00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:33.469
Now, there are a number of different ways

51
00:02:33.469 --> 00:02:36.370
to calculate visual acuity.

52
00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:38.050
I have two examples here.

53
00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:40.449
One of them is by Planar LED.

54
00:02:40.449 --> 00:02:43.770
They have a specific set of guidelines here.

55
00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:48.610
So they have the pixel pitch times a specific one arc minute

56
00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:51.610
to give you the precise retina distance.

57
00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:55.530
If you have 20, 20 vision, this is in millimeters.

58
00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:58.449
It's at that point that the viewer can no longer

59
00:02:58.449 --> 00:03:02.330
distinguish individual pixels with his eye.

60
00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:04.770
The pixel pitch times 10,

61
00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:07.129
this is a more of a rounded way of doing it,

62
00:03:07.129 --> 00:03:10.560
is the approximate visual acuity distance.

63
00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.080
So if you look at the chart on the left,

64
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.639
you can see a number of different pixel pitch sizes.

65
00:03:15.639 --> 00:03:18.759
So if we took 2.5 millimeters,

66
00:03:18.759 --> 00:03:21.800
they have a specific distance to the wall

67
00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:24.199
for your best visual acuity distance

68
00:03:24.199 --> 00:03:27.639
and your average comfortable viewing distance.

69
00:03:27.639 --> 00:03:28.840
Christie, on the other hand,

70
00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:31.800
they have a slightly different suggestion.

71
00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.919
They have pixel pitch times eight,

72
00:03:33.919 --> 00:03:36.259
giving you the optimal viewing distance

73
00:03:36.259 --> 00:03:38.919
and pixel pitch times 3.28

74
00:03:38.919 --> 00:03:40.599
for the minimum viewing distance,

75
00:03:40.599 --> 00:03:44.639
meaning how potentially close you can be to the wall

76
00:03:44.639 --> 00:03:48.400
without being able to individually distinguish pixels.

77
00:03:48.919 --> 00:03:51.319
When you're applying this to your camera,

78
00:03:51.319 --> 00:03:53.879
this is something that you should realize

79
00:03:53.879 --> 00:03:58.560
because the pixel pitch is gonna determine really

80
00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:00.699
the best and most optimal place

81
00:04:00.699 --> 00:04:03.639
to place your camera within your facility.

82
00:04:03.639 --> 00:04:06.340
You can shoot obviously further away,

83
00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:08.719
but when you start getting too close to the wall,

84
00:04:08.719 --> 00:04:11.159
you're gonna potentially introduce more and more,

85
00:04:11.159 --> 00:04:13.219
and you'll definitely start seeing

86
00:04:13.219 --> 00:04:15.159
the individual pixel elements.

87
00:04:15.159 --> 00:04:18.240
So this will also depend a lot

88
00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:23.420
on what type of camera sensor size you're using

89
00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:26.339
and the lens that you'll be using as well.

90
00:04:26.339 --> 00:04:29.279
So next I wanna talk a little bit

91
00:04:29.279 --> 00:04:32.420
about power considerations and flicker.

92
00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:36.220
So artificial lights, if you have them in your facility,

93
00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:38.220
which I'm sure you do,

94
00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:40.540
they're gonna be introducing a certain amount

95
00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:44.379
of potential for flicker during your shooting process,

96
00:04:44.379 --> 00:04:48.500
in addition to what may happen on your actual wall itself.

97
00:04:49.019 --> 00:04:53.420
So all artificial lights and all walls

98
00:04:53.420 --> 00:04:57.060
utilize alternating current in order to power them,

99
00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:59.139
and this is prone to flicker

100
00:04:59.139 --> 00:05:01.379
under certain types of conditions.

101
00:05:01.379 --> 00:05:05.620
So in the world, we have different primary regions

102
00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:09.139
that have two primary operating currents,

103
00:05:09.139 --> 00:05:13.360
60 hertz in the US and 50 hertz in the UK.

104
00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:16.459
Flicker will occur when there is a mismatch

105
00:05:16.459 --> 00:05:18.740
between the power operating frequencies

106
00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:20.660
and the screen refresh rate

107
00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:23.699
and the camera shutter speed and shutter angle.

108
00:05:23.699 --> 00:05:26.660
So these timing problems will eventually

109
00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:31.120
and potentially give you what's being shown on this slide,

110
00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:32.980
a little bit of strange flickering

111
00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:36.870
or black banding that can occur.

112
00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:39.209
Now, in order to help counter that,

113
00:05:39.209 --> 00:05:41.410
there's some things that you should keep in mind,

114
00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:43.949
and we're gonna go over that in just a second,

115
00:05:43.949 --> 00:05:46.829
but there's also the issue of frame rate.

116
00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:49.509
This is the number of frames per second

117
00:05:49.509 --> 00:05:50.829
within your source video

118
00:05:50.829 --> 00:05:54.959
that you're going to be transacting to the wall.

119
00:05:54.959 --> 00:05:57.519
That can be either at 24 frames per second,

120
00:05:57.519 --> 00:05:59.040
25 frames per second,

121
00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:03.500
2997, 30, 48, 50, or 60 frames per second,

122
00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:05.560
whether you're shooting in either a film

123
00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:07.720
or broadcast type environment.

124
00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:10.240
The refresh rate, on the other hand,

125
00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:12.680
this is the number of times per second

126
00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:16.360
a frame is redrawn on the display itself.

127
00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:18.480
So this is measured in Hertz,

128
00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:22.740
and LED walls are typically manufactured

129
00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:26.759
of having what are called camera safe refresh rates.

130
00:06:26.759 --> 00:06:30.959
And these are at 1,920 Hertz,

131
00:06:30.959 --> 00:06:35.959
3,840 Hertz, or 7,680 Hertz.

132
00:06:36.259 --> 00:06:37.879
If you are operating within

133
00:06:37.879 --> 00:06:40.779
one of those camera safe refresh rates,

134
00:06:40.779 --> 00:06:43.480
you're gonna be pretty good to go

135
00:06:43.519 --> 00:06:46.439
for a number of different shooting frame rates

136
00:06:46.439 --> 00:06:49.040
that you might want to be operating at.

137
00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:50.279
All of those frame rates

138
00:06:50.279 --> 00:06:54.259
are all factors of the 60 power Hertz frequency.

139
00:06:54.259 --> 00:06:57.000
Now, what can cause problems?

140
00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.879
I'm showing there on the right there,

141
00:06:58.879 --> 00:07:00.939
symptoms of low refresh rate,

142
00:07:00.939 --> 00:07:02.879
black banding in cameras,

143
00:07:02.879 --> 00:07:05.060
inconsistent or blotchy appearances,

144
00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:07.579
like the color is looking wrong

145
00:07:07.579 --> 00:07:10.540
and blotchy throughout your wall.

146
00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:12.720
You could have ghosting issues,

147
00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:15.519
color mosaic patterns, like I said,

148
00:07:15.519 --> 00:07:19.860
flickering and even potentially tearing of the display.

149
00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:22.639
So if you're putting together

150
00:07:22.639 --> 00:07:26.399
potentially one of your own independent solutions,

151
00:07:26.399 --> 00:07:28.600
you wanna be very careful

152
00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:31.040
on what type of panels you pick,

153
00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:33.480
because a number of different video panels

154
00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:35.600
may be rated to display video,

155
00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:40.220
but may not be at a proper camera safe refresh rate.

156
00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:42.660
And if you have one of those potential panels,

157
00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:46.100
in your wall, you will know it and you'll see it.

158
00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:47.579
So make sure that the panels

159
00:07:47.579 --> 00:07:50.060
that you purchase for your facility

160
00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:52.620
have one of these camera refresh rate

161
00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:56.319
that are safe for your panels.

162
00:07:56.319 --> 00:07:59.680
Now, rolling shutter versus global shutters.

163
00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:02.600
We talked about cameras having one or the other.

164
00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:05.990
Let's talk about what that is.

165
00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:07.709
The camera type of shutter,

166
00:08:07.709 --> 00:08:12.709
it will impact and potentially produce temporal artifacts

167
00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:16.350
that you might see during recording

168
00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:20.430
if your sensor size or your shutter type isn't ideal.

169
00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:23.189
So the sensor size and the camera processing power

170
00:08:23.189 --> 00:08:26.410
will determine how fast a rolling shutter

171
00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:29.750
can potentially capture an image

172
00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:31.569
on the actual sensor itself.

173
00:08:31.569 --> 00:08:33.450
So if you can see by the example here,

174
00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:38.450
the rolling shutter will basically capture the image,

175
00:08:38.669 --> 00:08:40.649
scan line by scan line.

176
00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:47.240
And if the camera sensor size is large,

177
00:08:47.559 --> 00:08:50.080
like a full frame sensor,

178
00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:52.279
it can take potentially a little longer

179
00:08:52.279 --> 00:08:57.659
to capture all of the image via a rolling shutter.

180
00:08:57.659 --> 00:09:02.620
Smaller sensor sizes like APS-C

181
00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:07.840
or micro four thirds or two thirds inch sensors,

182
00:09:08.039 --> 00:09:09.240
because they're smaller,

183
00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:13.679
a rolling shutter doesn't impact them as much,

184
00:09:13.679 --> 00:09:16.240
but they still can have problems.

185
00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:18.159
And what may happen with a rolling shutter

186
00:09:18.159 --> 00:09:21.139
is that while you're panning or moving the camera,

187
00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:26.320
items that are like very vertical in nature,

188
00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:29.720
imagery wise, can potentially start to bend

189
00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:31.879
or look like they're warping.

190
00:09:31.879 --> 00:09:34.559
And that's something that is an artifact

191
00:09:34.559 --> 00:09:36.360
of using a rolling shutter.

192
00:09:36.399 --> 00:09:39.960
Now a global shutter on the other hand, it's different.

193
00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:42.279
The global shutter will actually capture

194
00:09:42.279 --> 00:09:46.440
the entire image on the sensor in one snap.

195
00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:51.529
So this is an advantage for ICV effects walls,

196
00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:56.730
because it helps to really mitigate

197
00:09:56.730 --> 00:10:00.169
a number of potential issues that you might encounter,

198
00:10:00.169 --> 00:10:03.000
especially with Moir.

199
00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:05.519
So these camera safe LED refresh rates

200
00:10:05.559 --> 00:10:09.919
along with Genlock, your house Genlock,

201
00:10:09.919 --> 00:10:12.159
this can help counter some of the problems

202
00:10:12.159 --> 00:10:13.720
with rolling shutter.

203
00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:17.669
But if you really want to be certain,

204
00:10:17.669 --> 00:10:19.570
go for global shutter.

205
00:10:19.570 --> 00:10:23.129
Next, we wanna talk about the LED refresh shutter angle

206
00:10:23.129 --> 00:10:24.929
and shutter speed issues.

207
00:10:24.929 --> 00:10:27.409
So if you're uncertain,

208
00:10:27.409 --> 00:10:32.250
whether your walls refresh rate frequency is adequate,

209
00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:33.389
or you're planning on shooting

210
00:10:33.389 --> 00:10:35.389
an additional LED based lights

211
00:10:35.389 --> 00:10:38.549
with various pulse width modulation

212
00:10:38.549 --> 00:10:42.389
on the way it handles generating light,

213
00:10:43.559 --> 00:10:48.000
you wanna make sure that you aren't getting any flicker

214
00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:50.720
from these additional external lights,

215
00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:54.519
because they can introduce it into your shooting process.

216
00:10:54.519 --> 00:10:57.120
So there is a website that you can go to.

217
00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:00.360
It's actually from Red Camera.

218
00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:02.399
This particular website will allow you

219
00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:06.480
to plug in the power frequency that you're operating at

220
00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:08.639
and the frame rate that you're using.

221
00:11:08.639 --> 00:11:10.960
And when you click on calculate,

222
00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:15.639
it will actually supply you with the shutter speed

223
00:11:16.639 --> 00:11:21.769
or the shutter angle that's best for countering flicker.

224
00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:27.870
So anytime that you change the frame rate on your camera,

225
00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:29.870
it's something to be aware of.

226
00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:32.509
Now, most of the time when you're shooting in an LED wall,

227
00:11:33.029 --> 00:11:36.110
you're probably not gonna be variating your frame rate,

228
00:11:36.110 --> 00:11:38.909
but in case you have to,

229
00:11:38.909 --> 00:11:41.570
this is something that you need to pay attention to.

230
00:11:41.570 --> 00:11:43.190
So I've got it down there.

231
00:11:43.190 --> 00:11:46.110
Changing camera frame rates when shooting LED walls

232
00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:48.269
can also be problematic.

233
00:11:48.269 --> 00:11:50.909
LED walls that can't support frame rates

234
00:11:50.909 --> 00:11:54.029
higher than 48 frames per second at this time.

235
00:11:54.029 --> 00:11:55.990
And this is something to be aware of.

236
00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:58.990
There are newer panels that are coming out

237
00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:01.690
and newer cameras that will support higher frame rates.

238
00:12:01.710 --> 00:12:04.490
But for now, you should either be operating

239
00:12:04.490 --> 00:12:07.450
at a frame rate of 24 frames per second,

240
00:12:07.450 --> 00:12:09.370
or if you're in a broadcast environment,

241
00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:13.970
you'll probably be doing 29.97 or 30 frames per second.

242
00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:16.889
And then you can also potentially opt

243
00:12:16.889 --> 00:12:19.929
to go at 48 frames per second.

244
00:12:19.929 --> 00:12:22.490
I've talked a lot about Moyer mitigation.

245
00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:25.509
Let's get into what that actually is.

246
00:12:25.509 --> 00:12:27.919
So what is Moyer?

247
00:12:27.919 --> 00:12:30.519
Well, this is an interference pattern

248
00:12:30.519 --> 00:12:33.879
that's produced from photographing and overlapping

249
00:12:33.879 --> 00:12:38.960
various lower resolution LED panels

250
00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:41.879
versus the camera's sensor grid.

251
00:12:41.879 --> 00:12:47.240
So the camera sensor has a number of photo cells on it

252
00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:49.720
just like everything else.

253
00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:52.960
And it's like, it's capturing light information,

254
00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:57.960
and it has to have this laid out in a grid.

255
00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:00.320
And the higher the resolution,

256
00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:04.779
your sensor of your camera is, the tighter that grid is.

257
00:13:04.779 --> 00:13:09.000
And higher resolution sensors are actually gonna be helpful

258
00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:12.120
in mitigating Moyer when shooting

259
00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:15.740
against a lower resolution grid of an LED wall.

260
00:13:16.799 --> 00:13:18.919
As mentioned, the LED wall,

261
00:13:18.919 --> 00:13:23.980
there's 172 or 192 pixels across and down.

262
00:13:25.179 --> 00:13:26.740
That's rather low resolution

263
00:13:26.740 --> 00:13:28.700
in comparison to a camera sensor.

264
00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:32.659
So higher resolution camera sensors, the better.

265
00:13:32.659 --> 00:13:33.620
There are other ways

266
00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:36.059
that you can potentially mitigate Moyer,

267
00:13:36.059 --> 00:13:39.580
and that may be changing the camera angle.

268
00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:42.960
So if you are trying to line up a shot

269
00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:45.340
and you're seeing that Moyer is happening,

270
00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:47.179
it could just be simply resolved

271
00:13:47.179 --> 00:13:50.779
by moving the camera slightly off access

272
00:13:50.779 --> 00:13:55.580
or potentially altering your camera angle just enough

273
00:13:55.580 --> 00:13:57.340
and you can get rid of that

274
00:13:57.340 --> 00:14:00.179
for a more of like a locked off shot.

275
00:14:00.179 --> 00:14:05.179
You can also potentially use a lower camera aperture.

276
00:14:05.639 --> 00:14:08.419
So as you can see across the display here,

277
00:14:08.419 --> 00:14:10.779
you've got different F-stops

278
00:14:10.779 --> 00:14:14.460
or on professional lenses, you have T-stops.

279
00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:17.100
These F-stops and T-stops will tell you

280
00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:20.659
just how large the camera aperture is

281
00:14:20.659 --> 00:14:22.059
and how wide it's open.

282
00:14:22.059 --> 00:14:26.860
So the lower the number, like an F1, an F1.4,

283
00:14:27.379 --> 00:14:30.740
or an F2, the bigger the opening of the aperture.

284
00:14:30.740 --> 00:14:34.899
Whereas the higher numbers like an F16 or an F22,

285
00:14:34.899 --> 00:14:38.179
it has a smaller aperture opening.

286
00:14:38.179 --> 00:14:43.179
So if you use an aperture of T2.8 or lower,

287
00:14:43.539 --> 00:14:45.340
and you have a wider opening,

288
00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:47.980
you're gonna have a shallower depth of field.

289
00:14:47.980 --> 00:14:50.220
And with this shallower depth of field,

290
00:14:50.220 --> 00:14:54.700
you can potentially defocus the image of the LED wall

291
00:14:54.700 --> 00:14:56.620
behind your central actor.

292
00:14:57.220 --> 00:15:01.870
And this can be a big advantage of helping to reduce Moir.

293
00:15:01.870 --> 00:15:05.230
Speaking of depth of field, there's another great website.

294
00:15:05.230 --> 00:15:07.570
It's this depth of field simulator.

295
00:15:07.570 --> 00:15:11.470
It allows you to actually set up different settings

296
00:15:11.470 --> 00:15:13.590
and scenarios, and it does a great job

297
00:15:13.590 --> 00:15:15.870
of approximating what the depth of field

298
00:15:15.870 --> 00:15:18.110
would look like on a website.

299
00:15:18.110 --> 00:15:23.240
So the issue of depth of field is something that,

300
00:15:23.399 --> 00:15:27.799
as mentioned, is very helpful for Moir mitigation.

301
00:15:27.799 --> 00:15:31.120
So if you can slightly defocus the image

302
00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:33.279
that's behind your central actor,

303
00:15:33.279 --> 00:15:37.519
it will go a long way to making it look

304
00:15:37.519 --> 00:15:40.480
more professional and look more realistic.

305
00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:42.600
The whole depth of field, it's influenced

306
00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:45.120
by the lens aperture, as mentioned,

307
00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:47.440
your field of view, the sensor size,

308
00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:50.419
and the subject proximity to the camera.

309
00:15:50.419 --> 00:15:53.179
So very narrow fields of view

310
00:15:53.179 --> 00:15:55.860
with the lower aperture settings,

311
00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:58.279
with the wider opening of the aperture,

312
00:15:58.279 --> 00:16:01.100
will make that depth of field very shallow,

313
00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:04.379
and you can place your subject in focus

314
00:16:04.379 --> 00:16:06.860
while everything behind it is not.

315
00:16:06.860 --> 00:16:10.279
Next, I wanna talk about other ways

316
00:16:10.279 --> 00:16:13.360
of dealing with potential Moir,

317
00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:17.720
and that is using an optical low-pass filter,

318
00:16:17.720 --> 00:16:21.679
or some people will call this an anti-aliasing filter.

319
00:16:21.679 --> 00:16:24.460
Every camera in front of the sensor

320
00:16:24.460 --> 00:16:27.840
has an option of, well, not every camera,

321
00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:30.519
but number of cameras have the option

322
00:16:30.519 --> 00:16:32.980
of installing an optical low-pass filter

323
00:16:32.980 --> 00:16:34.480
in front of the sensor.

324
00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:36.340
Others have them already built in.

325
00:16:36.340 --> 00:16:40.120
So essentially, what it is is this.

326
00:16:40.120 --> 00:16:43.360
It is a small device that's designed

327
00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:46.340
to attach in front of the sensor,

328
00:16:46.340 --> 00:16:49.500
and it will slightly soften the image

329
00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:53.279
in order to help reduce Moir patterns,

330
00:16:53.279 --> 00:16:57.039
especially from various different grids

331
00:16:57.039 --> 00:17:00.360
or things that have really high-frequency patterns

332
00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:01.659
and so forth.

333
00:17:01.659 --> 00:17:06.420
This is the Blackmagic URSA camera,

334
00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:10.339
the G2 that we're shooting on here at the Latte stage.

335
00:17:10.339 --> 00:17:13.299
It doesn't have an optical low-pass filter built in.

336
00:17:13.299 --> 00:17:16.259
It is something that has to be added to the camera,

337
00:17:16.900 --> 00:17:18.819
whereas my Panasonic BGH1,

338
00:17:18.819 --> 00:17:21.900
it has one built in in front of the sensor.

339
00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:25.099
So it just depends upon the camera manufacturer.

340
00:17:25.099 --> 00:17:26.460
You need to check the specs

341
00:17:26.460 --> 00:17:28.980
to see if it's something that's there or not,

342
00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:30.819
and if you want one, add one.

343
00:17:30.819 --> 00:17:32.690
It will help.

344
00:17:32.690 --> 00:17:36.670
Next, there's the aspect of using a neutral density filter.

345
00:17:36.670 --> 00:17:38.210
Now, these are devices

346
00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:41.049
that can either be built into the camera,

347
00:17:41.049 --> 00:17:44.809
or they can be attached to your lens,

348
00:17:44.809 --> 00:17:47.109
and these neutral density filters

349
00:17:47.109 --> 00:17:50.089
effectively block out a certain amount of light,

350
00:17:50.089 --> 00:17:54.170
allowing you to open up your aperture even more,

351
00:17:54.170 --> 00:17:56.470
thus reducing your depth of field

352
00:17:56.470 --> 00:17:59.170
and allowing you to have a blurry

353
00:17:59.170 --> 00:18:01.769
or a more blurred background.

354
00:18:01.769 --> 00:18:04.890
So these neutral density filters,

355
00:18:04.890 --> 00:18:07.730
especially considering that you're shooting

356
00:18:07.730 --> 00:18:10.549
in an environment with a lot of artificial light

357
00:18:10.549 --> 00:18:12.769
and a lot of bright light sources,

358
00:18:12.769 --> 00:18:16.329
normally a lot of people will reduce the size

359
00:18:16.329 --> 00:18:18.150
of the aperture in order to prevent

360
00:18:18.150 --> 00:18:19.609
as much light coming in,

361
00:18:19.609 --> 00:18:21.930
or they'll change the ISO of the camera

362
00:18:21.930 --> 00:18:24.940
to accommodate for that as well.

363
00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:26.980
Perhaps using a neutral density filter

364
00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:28.339
would be better for this.

365
00:18:28.339 --> 00:18:31.259
That way you can have a lot of bright light

366
00:18:31.259 --> 00:18:35.140
in your scene, but you can shoot with a wider aperture,

367
00:18:35.140 --> 00:18:37.339
thus reducing your depth of field

368
00:18:37.339 --> 00:18:41.880
and giving you that very important blurry background.

369
00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:46.539
On the Blackmagic URSA camera, encircled there in red,

370
00:18:47.519 --> 00:18:50.019
its neutral density filters are actually built in,

371
00:18:50.019 --> 00:18:53.220
so you can actually just turn a knob on the camera

372
00:18:53.220 --> 00:18:54.720
and you can switch through, I think,

373
00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:58.339
four different settings on this particular camera,

374
00:18:58.339 --> 00:19:00.819
each one changing it by probably one

375
00:19:00.819 --> 00:19:04.680
or two stops per filter.

376
00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:07.000
Next, there's the issue of shutter speed,

377
00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:09.299
and shutter speed can also help you

378
00:19:09.299 --> 00:19:12.420
address the whole Moir problem as well.

379
00:19:13.339 --> 00:19:16.660
At faster shutter speeds, obviously,

380
00:19:16.660 --> 00:19:19.420
you're gonna be able to capture the action quicker,

381
00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:23.420
whereas slower shutter speeds are going to cause

382
00:19:23.420 --> 00:19:26.660
a little bit of a blurring effect

383
00:19:26.660 --> 00:19:28.299
to what you're shooting.

384
00:19:28.299 --> 00:19:32.119
Depending upon your situation,

385
00:19:32.119 --> 00:19:34.339
adjusting the shutter speed can be something

386
00:19:34.339 --> 00:19:37.269
that you can do to slightly, again,

387
00:19:37.269 --> 00:19:40.190
mitigate Moir, soften the image a little bit,

388
00:19:40.230 --> 00:19:42.549
introduce a little bit of motion blur,

389
00:19:42.549 --> 00:19:44.910
and that too can go a long way,

390
00:19:44.910 --> 00:19:46.269
especially when combining it

391
00:19:46.269 --> 00:19:48.990
with all of these other different approaches.

392
00:19:48.990 --> 00:19:51.710
Finally, if you still capture an image

393
00:19:51.710 --> 00:19:54.890
and you've noticed Moir still cropping up

394
00:19:54.890 --> 00:19:56.609
into the final product,

395
00:19:56.609 --> 00:19:59.670
you can remove it via software in post.

396
00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:01.670
It's not usually ideal.

397
00:20:01.670 --> 00:20:04.910
It can cause a little bit of softening

398
00:20:04.910 --> 00:20:06.710
of the rest of the image.

399
00:20:06.710 --> 00:20:08.289
It can cause some other problems,

400
00:20:08.289 --> 00:20:13.289
but it is possible to use software to rectify the problem.

401
00:20:14.130 --> 00:20:16.049
Of course, that will take additional time,

402
00:20:16.049 --> 00:20:19.089
but when you have to do it, you have to do it.

403
00:20:19.089 --> 00:20:21.170
All right, this concludes our lecture

404
00:20:21.170 --> 00:20:23.450
on camera configuration and hardware

405
00:20:23.450 --> 00:20:26.369
for the ICD-FX volume and stage.

406
00:20:26.369 --> 00:20:28.369
If you have any particular questions,

407
00:20:28.369 --> 00:20:30.450
we'll answer them during our Q&A period

408
00:20:30.450 --> 00:20:31.710
following this lecture.

409
00:20:31.710 --> 00:20:32.549
Thanks.
